= Installing Oracle 9iR2 on Redhat 9 =

== Configure the Kernel ==

Oracle 9i requires that you at least temporarily increase the maximum shared memory segment size for installation. To temporarily increase the maximum shared memory segment size, follow these instructions (as root):

'''echo "1073741824" &gt; /proc/sys/kernel/shmmax'''

== Set Up the Environment ==

Add the following line to /home/oracle/.bashrc near the bottom:

'''export LD_ASSUME_KERNEL=2.4.1'''

== Install Compatibility Packages ==

The Apache web server that comes with Oracle 9i requires Berkeley DB version 3. Redhat 9 comes with Berkeley DB version 4. However, there is a compatibility RPM available:

'''compat-db-3.3.11-4.i386.rpm'''

You can download it from [http://rpmfind.net rpmfind] and install it using //rpm -i// as root.

Some versions of Redhat 9 came with the following glibc RPM:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
glibc-2.3.2-5.i386.rpm
}}}
}}}

Others came with:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
glibc-2.3.2-11.9.i386.rpm
}}}
}}}

The first one works but the second one is broken.

If you have run up2date (or apt-get upgrade if you're using apt) then it is likely that even if you once had a working glibc, you no longer do.

To assure that Oracle will install and run, download and install [http://www.firstworks.com/support/libctypefixup-1.0.tar.gz libctypefixup] using the following commands (as root).

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
tar xf libctypefixup-1.0.tar.gz
cd libctypefixup-1.0
make
make install
}}}
}}}

== Install Oracle ==

Now, log in as the oracle user.

Since Oracle 9.2.0 uses a graphical installer, you need to be running X Windows or point the DISPLAY environment variable to an X Windows session running somewhere.

I have had problems with the graphical installer on enlightenment and fluxbox window managers in the past, though they may work for you.

If you have a CD, mount it and cd to the mount point. If you have a cpio distribution, extract all three files using:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
cpio -idmv < lnx_920_disk1.cpio
cpio -idmv < lnx_920_disk2.cpio
cpio -idmv < lnx_920_disk3.cpio
}}}
}}}

Change directories to the Disk1 directory.[[br]][[br]] Type '''./runInstaller'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Welcome''' screen click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] An '''Inventory Location''' dialog will pop up.[[br]]Accept the default base directory and click '''OK'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Unix Group Name''' screen, enter //oinstall// for the '''UNIX Group Name''' and click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] A dialog will pop up and prompt you to run /tmp/orainstRoot.sh as root.[[br]]Follow the instructions and click '''Continue'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''File Locations''' screen, accept the defaults and click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Available Products''' screen, accept the default selection '''Oracle9i Database 9.2.0.1.0''' and click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Installation Types''' screen, accept the default selection '''Enterprise Edition''' and click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Database Configuration''' screen, accept the default selection '''General Purpose''' and click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Database Identification''' screen, enter //ora1// for both '''Global Database Name''' and '''SID''' and click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Database File Location''' screen, accept the default '''Directory for Database Files''' and click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Database Character Set''' screen, accept the default selection '''Use the default character set''' and click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Summary''' screen, click '''Install'''[[br]][[br]] The '''Install''' screen will show the progress of the installation[[br]][[br]] If a dialog pops up indicating:

Error in invoking target install of makefile[[br]]/u01/app/oracle/product/9.2.0/network/lib/ins_oemagent.mk edit /u01/app/oracle/product/9.2.0/network/lib/env_oemagent.mk and change:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
 LDLIBS=$(EXPDLIBS) $(EXOSLIBS) $(SYSLIBS) $(EXSYSLIBS) $(MATHLIB) $(USRLIBS)
}}}
}}}

to:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
 LDLIBS=$(EXPDLIBS) $(EXOSLIBS) $(SYSLIBS) $(EXSYSLIBS) $(MATHLIB) $(USRLIBS) -lctypefixup
}}}
}}}

then click '''Retry'''[[br]][[br]][[br]] If a dialog pops up indicating:

Error in invoking target install of makefile[[br]]/u01/app/oracle/product/9.2.0/ctx/lib/ins_ctx.mk edit /u01/app/oracle/product/9.2.0/ctx/lib/ins_ctx.mk and change:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
ctxhx: $(CTXHXOBJ)
        $(LINK) $(CTXHXOBJ) $(INSO_LINK)
}}}
}}}

to:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
ctxhx: $(CTXHXOBJ)
        $(LINK) $(CTXHXOBJ) $(INSO_LINK) -ldl
}}}
}}}

then click '''Retry'''

At the end of the installation, a dialog will pop up asking you to run root.sh. Just follow the directions on the dialog and click '''Ok'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Configuration Tools''' screen, the Database Creation Assistant will run and a dialog will most likely pop up, indicating that it failed. In that case, click Ok on the dialog and, run the following command as root:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
touch /etc/rac_on
}}}
}}}

Now select '''Oracle Database Configuration Assistant''' And click '''Retry'''[[br]][[br]] A dialog will pop up indicating that a database is being created. After a while, another dialog will pop up instructing you to provide passwords for the SYS and SYSTEM users. Enter new passwords for these users. Note that you cannont use the classic "manager" and "change_on_install" passwords.[[br]]Click '''OK'''[[br]][[br]] Sometimes the Database Creation Assistant will just hang here. Select '''Oracle Database Configuration Assistant''' And click Stop. If it doesn't stop, you may have to kill it from the command line using the following command:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{
kill `ps -efa | grep "JDBC_PROTOCOL=thin" | cut -c10-15`
}}}
}}}

When the Database Creation Assistant is finished (or killed), click Next.[[br]][[br]] On the '''End Of Installation''' screen click '''Exit'''

In some cases, dbstart and dbshut don't work after installation. To ensure that they will work, you need to run the following command:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
cp /u01/app/oracle/admin/ora1/pfile/initora1.ora.* /u01/app/oracle/product/9.2.0/dbs/initora1.ora
}}}
}}}

